comments.body:
`new Date(value)` as the fallback silently rescues ISO-shaped strings that `parseISO` correctly rejected: `parseDate("2024-02-30")` returns **March 1**, and `dateQueryString`/`dateTimeQueryString` then emit a confident-looking filter for input that was never a real date. Under Day.js these came back invalid and the callers returned `""`.

The fallback only exists for non-ISO shapes like `Date.toString()` output (your own test at `date.test.ts` covers exactly that). Gate it so ISO-shaped strings stay invalid:

```ts
const ISO_SHAPED = /^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}/;
const date = parseISO(value);
if (isValid(date)) return date;
return ISO_SHAPED.test(value) ? new Date(NaN) : new Date(value);
```

(Copilot raised this too — flagging it because it's the one behavior change here that produces a wrong value rather than wrong wording.)
